[{"learnocaml_version":"2","kind":"exercise","title":"Hailstone Sequences","short_description":"Count steps in an iterative sequence","stars":1,"identifier":"collatz","authors":[["Max Kopinsky","max.kopinsky@mcgill.ca"],["Jacob Errington","jacob.errington@mcgill.ca"]]},{"id":"collatz","prelude":"","template":"let hailstone (n : int) : int = failwith \"TODO\"\n","descr":[["","<h1 id=\"hailstone-sequences\">Hailstone Sequences</h1>\n<p><em>Hailstone sequences</em> are defined by iterating the following\nfunction:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>H(n) = n/2 if n is even</li>\n<li>H(n) = 3n+1 if n is odd</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, the sequence for 5 begins with 5, then H(5) = 16, then\nH(16) = 8, then H(8) = 4, H(4) = 2, H(2) = 1, etc. Once a sequence\nreaches 1, it become stuck in the loop 1 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 1.</p>\n<p>Your task is to count the number of steps it takes for the input\nvalue <code>n</code> to reach 1. The result for 5 should be 5. You may\nuse any recursive style you like (but do be aware of stack overflow if\nyou choose not to use tail recursion).</p>\n<h1 id=\"aside\">Aside</h1>\n<p>These sequences are the subject of one of the most famous open\nproblems in mathematics, the <em>Collatz Conjecture</em>. The conjecture\nis that every starting value eventually reaches 1, as opposed to getting\nstuck in a different loop or growing forever.</p>\n<p>The Collatz conjecture has been open for 86 years and work on it has\nspawned lots of interesting math, if you want to check that out.</p>\n"]],"prepare":"1LfBO04w1NdVCJqhGMqCNyVKXkt+C5tS359TPmx=","test":"","solution":"LGRYZq89RyRfcFhb9MCirzN0TQV4TRAZ1oBYWlSgc3Ier5khAWCJG8BhfrOcrONroCzb4jNnfrqyXg1RqnrGq6ZbRoc7UfpL74RavM6TI4IhCJUBp4h3bDoHFtVC5SLQCNfUaZ1AFuSTPnu+Tm9Gc3KBu9+BCoXtfBhJ7YmBcDU8K6kqtKl1Mz35GyDEnZXlYp5DTFZWGQ/wX8tVOuy5p8qgCXp0Jgg4JmD9aejtvjWKNN5fI7C9e3I3/Fj74N8pRWICA8ZRkFbJay0H","max-score":10,"depend":null,"dependencies":[]},null]